thread:
ashley: quick check — running into a leak when cancelling streaming generation requests from the Go SDK. ctx cancel doesn't close the underlying http2 stream; we end up with goroutine piles. anyone seen this pattern?

matt: yep saw similar last month. did you use req = req.WithContext(ctx)? that usually wires cancellation through. also are you closing resp.Body after cancel?

ashley: yep using WithContext and calling cancel. code path is streaming tokens back via resp.Body. on cancel I call resp.Body.Close() but goroutines still linger (http2 stream reads stuck).

sara: h2 has a weird semantics: if server is mid-frame you can get stuck reads. we mitigated by calling transport.CloseIdleConnections() from the cancel handler to nudge connection pool. not ideal but helped.

lee: adding: check Transport settings. we set MaxIdleConnsPerHost to 100 and IdleConnTimeout to 90s; for heavy streaming that can keep connections around. lowering IdleConnTimeout helped us. also ForceAttemptHTTP2=true when using custom net/http.Transport + http2.ConfigureTransport.

toni: fyi we found that if you don't drain the response body after cancel some net/http internals will block. pattern that worked for us for streaming cancellations: in the cancel path do a goroutine that closes resp.Body and also calls transport.CloseIdleConnections() — weird but pragmatic.

ashley: can you paste an example snippet?

matt: sure — simplified:

```go
tr := &http.Transport{
    MaxIdleConns:        200,
    MaxIdleConnsPerHost: 40,
    IdleConnTimeout:     30 * time.Second,
    // TLS / dial config...
}
http2.ConfigureTransport(tr) // enables http2 on net/http
client := &http.Client{Transport: tr, Timeout: 0}

req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, "GET", url, nil)
resp, err := client.Do(req)
if err != nil { return err }
// when cancelling:
go func() {
    <-ctx.Done()
    // ensure body closed to release read goroutine
    resp.Body.Close()
    // nudge pool
    tr.CloseIdleConnections()
}()

// stream read loop
buf := make([]byte, 4096)
for {
    n, err := resp.Body.Read(buf)
    if n > 0 { process(buf[:n]) }
    if err != nil { break }
}
```

ashley: thanks. curious if we should change SDK behavior — currently SDK retries streaming requests on 5xx, which probably makes things worse. maybe don't retry if request had output streamed.

sara: agreed — streaming retries are dangerous. Recommend: only retry if no bytes were delivered to the caller. otherwise surfacing an error is safer.

lee: +1. also add an internal metric that counts "streaming retry" occurrences so we can detect this pattern.

toni: question on timeouts: do we want per-request deadline or rely on transport-level idle timeouts? per-request deadlines are better because they bound goroutine lifetimes tightly.

matt: yep use context with timeout per request. transport IdleConnTimeout is for idle keepalives between requests. not a substitute for request deadlines.

ashley: i'll open a tiny RFC/PR: (1) don't auto-retry streaming responses after any bytes emitted, (2) ensure SDK calls resp.Body.Close() on cancel and triggers CloseIdleConnections(), (3) docs about using request timeouts. will ping for review.

